<h2 id="purpose">What the State Pension is for</h2>

<p>The State Pension gives a basic income in later life. It sits beside workplace and personal pensions, which people build up through their own and their employer's contributions. The Pensions Commission describes it as the base that other retirement saving is built on. {{ m.c("pc-firm-foundation") }}</p>

<p>Most pensioners have more than one source of income. {{ m.f("share-with-state-pension") }} of pensioner units receive the State Pension, and {{ m.f("share-with-private-pension") }} receive a private pension. {{ m.f("share-state-only") }} have only the State Pension and other state benefits.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("share-state-only" | figure) }}

<h2 id="which-system">What your date of birth decides</h2>

<p>There are two systems. Date of birth decides which one a person is on and their State Pension age. For people born between <time datetime="1951-04-06">6 April 1951</time> and <time datetime="1953-04-05">5 April 1953</time>, which system also depends on whether they are a man or a woman.</p>

