<h2 id="tax-and-payment">Tax and how it is paid</h2>

<p>The State Pension is taxable, but it is paid without tax taken off. {{ m.c("taxable-paid-gross") }} Tax that is due is usually collected through the tax code on a private pension or on earnings. If it cannot be collected that way, HM Revenue and Customs sends a bill. {{ m.c("simple-assessment") }}</p>

<p>The full new State Pension over 52 weeks is {{ m.f("nsp-full-52-weeks") }}. {{ m.ref(("nsp-full-52-weeks" | figure).source) }} The standard Personal Allowance is {{ m.f("personal-allowance") }} a year, so the full pension is just below it. A person with a protected payment, extra pension from deferring, or any other income can have tax to pay.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("personal-allowance" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("Announced, not yet law", 3) %}
<p>{{ ("simple-assessment-2027" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("simple-assessment-2027") }}</p>
<p>{{ ("pm-speech-income-tax" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("pm-speech-income-tax") }} The speech is as published by the Labour Party. GOV.UK carries no transcript.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>The State Pension is usually paid every 4 weeks, in arrears. {{ m.c("paid-four-weekly") }} {{ m.c("paid-in-arrears") }}</p>

{% call m.note("A 4-week payment is not a month's money", 3) %}
<p>There are 13 four-week periods in 52 weeks. A payment of 4 weeks at the full new rate is {{ (("nsp-full-weekly" | figPence) * 4) | money }}. Spread over 12 calendar months, 52 weeks at that rate is {{ ((("nsp-full-weekly" | figPence) * 52) / 12) | round | money }} a month. Budgeting by the month with 4-week payments means one payment in the year falls in a month that already had one.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="extra-help">Pension Credit and other help</h2>

<p>These are separate from the State Pension, and each has its own rules. Some have to be claimed, and some are paid automatically.</p>

<h3>Pension Credit</h3>
<p>Pension Credit is a means-tested benefit for people over State Pension age on a low income. The part called Guarantee Credit tops up weekly income to {{ m.f("pension-credit-single") }} for a single person or {{ m.f("pension-credit-couple") }} for a couple. Extra amounts apply for severe disability and for carers.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pension-credit-single" | figure) }}

<p>{{ ("pc-mixed-age" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("pc-mixed-age") }}</p>
<p>Many people who are entitled do not claim. In the latest estimate, {{ m.f("pension-credit-take-up") }} of entitled families claimed. About {{ m.f("pension-credit-non-recipients") }} families did not, and {{ m.f("pension-credit-unclaimed") }} went unclaimed.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pension-credit-take-up" | figure) }}
<p><a href="https://www.gov.uk/pension-credit">Check whether you can get Pension Credit on GOV.UK</a>.</p>

<h3>Help with housing and Council Tax</h3>
<p>{{ ("housing-benefit" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("housing-benefit") }} {{ ("council-tax-reduction" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("council-tax-reduction") }} {{ ("council-tax-reduction-pensioners" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("council-tax-reduction-pensioners") }}</p>

<h3>Help with the costs of disability</h3>
<p>Attendance Allowance is for people over State Pension age who need help or supervision because of a disability or health condition. It is {{ m.f("attendance-allowance-lower") }} or {{ m.f("attendance-allowance-higher") }} a week and is not means-tested.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("attendance-allowance-lower" | figure) }}

<p>Attendance Allowance covers England and Wales. {{ m.c("aa-scotland") }} {{ ("aa-northern-ireland" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("aa-northern-ireland") }}</p>

<h3>Winter Fuel Payment</h3>
<p>The Winter Fuel Payment is paid to people over State Pension age in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. {{ ("wfp-automatic" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("wfp-automatic") }} For people with a total income over {{ m.f("winter-fuel-income-limit") }}, HM Revenue and Customs takes the payment back through the tax system.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("winter-fuel-income-limit" | figure) }}

<h2 id="financing">How it is paid for</h2>

<p>The State Pension is paid from the National Insurance Fund. {{ m.c("pay-as-you-go") }}</p>

<p>This is the main difference from a defined contribution workplace or personal pension. In those, contributions are invested in a pot that belongs to the saver. A defined benefit workplace pension is different again: it promises an income under the scheme's rules, usually linked to pay and years of service. In the State Pension there is no pot. A person's contributions build an entitlement under the rules, and the money paid in by today's workers and employers pays today's pensions.</p>

